WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.366
Vibe coding means using natural-language interaction to direct software creation and modification.

2
00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:09.838
Its value is not that the assistant replaces engineering judgment.

3
00:00:09.838 --> 00:00:16.994
Its value is that it can shorten the distance between an idea and a working experiment,

4
00:00:16.994 --> 00:00:23.702
especially when the developer keeps the goal clear and the change small enough to understand.

5
00:00:23.702 --> 00:00:26.385
The disciplined loop begins with intent.

6
00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:30.410
Define the outcome, scope, constraints, and observable success criteria.

7
00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:36.671
Supply the project context that matters: relevant files, tests, interfaces, errors, versions, and rules.

8
00:00:36.671 --> 00:00:45.615
If the task is broad or risky, ask for a plan before implementation so incorrect assumptions can be corrected early.

9
00:00:45.615 --> 00:00:48.298
Implementation should proceed through small changes.

10
00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:53.664
Inspect the diff, check that the files touched match the approved scope,

11
00:00:53.664 --> 00:00:59.478
and read the code at both the design level and the detail level.

12
00:00:59.478 --> 00:01:02.161
Run validation that fits the change.

13
00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:08.422
When something fails, debug from evidence: exact commands, clear errors, expected behavior, observed behavior,

14
00:01:08.422 --> 00:01:10.658
and the smallest reproducible case.

15
00:01:10.658 --> 00:01:16.472
Each pass should leave the project easier to reason about, not merely different.

16
00:01:16.472 --> 00:01:20.944
Security, privacy, dependencies, and maintainability remain part of every iteration.

17
00:01:20.944 --> 00:01:23.180
Protect secrets and restricted data.

18
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.969
Verify packages before installation.

19
00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:26.310
Limit tool permissions.

20
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.993
Use approval gates for consequential actions.

21
00:01:28.993 --> 00:01:35.254
When a prototype begins to matter, harden it with tests, documentation, configuration review, observability,

22
00:01:35.254 --> 00:01:37.490
deployment planning, and recovery thinking.

23
00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:41.068
Warning signs tell you when to slow down.

24
00:01:41.068 --> 00:01:47.329
The assistant may be editing files outside the intended scope, adding packages without justification,

25
00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:52.248
rewriting randomly after errors, or producing code the developer cannot explain.

26
00:01:52.248 --> 00:02:00.745
Tests may be skipped, private data may be exposed, or a prototype may be drifting toward production without review.

27
00:02:00.745 --> 00:02:04.770
These signals are not reasons to abandon AI assistance.

28
00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:10.583
They are reasons to narrow the task, gather evidence, and reassert human judgment.

29
00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:17.739
The closing principle is the one to carry forward: move quickly at the speed of generation,

30
00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:20.869
but decide at the speed of understanding.
